course: religion-ethics
lesson: 24
title: Baptism, communion and confirmation
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Christians call some special ceremonies sacraments: outward signs, using ordinary things like water, bread and wine, of God's grace at work inside; Catholics and Orthodox count seven, most Protestants two, baptism and communion.
explain: In baptism a person is washed with or dipped in water «in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit»; it is a sign of sin washed away, new life and joining the Church.
explain: Many churches baptise babies, with parents and godparents making promises, because God's love comes before we understand it; Baptists and many Pentecostals baptise only people old enough to choose, usually by full immersion; both sides give serious reasons.
explain: Holy Communion, also called the Eucharist, Mass or the Lord's Supper, shares bread and wine (or juice) as Jesus asked at the Last Supper; Catholics and Orthodox believe they truly become Christ's body and blood, while many Protestants see a holy remembrance.
explain: Catholic children often make their First Communion at about seven or eight, and in confirmation young people or adults make the baptism promises their own, often with a bishop laying hands on them.
explain: For a child today these steps show that faith is both a gift received and a choice made, and that people can disagree deeply about them and still respect each other.
fact: A sacrament is a church ceremony Christians believe is an outward sign of God's grace.
fact: Catholics and Orthodox count seven sacraments; most Protestants count two, baptism and communion.
fact: In baptism a person is washed with or dipped in water in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit.
fact: Baptism is a sign of washing away sin, starting a new life and joining the Church.
fact: Catholic, Orthodox, Anglican, Methodist and Presbyterian churches baptise babies.
fact: At an infant baptism, parents and godparents make promises to bring the child up in the faith.
fact: Baptists and many Pentecostal churches baptise people old enough to decide, usually by full immersion, which means going right under the water.
fact: Whether to baptise babies or only believers is a disputed question among Christians.
fact: Holy Communion is also called the Eucharist, the Mass or the Lord's Supper.
fact: Communion remembers Jesus' death, as he asked at the Last Supper.
fact: Catholics and Orthodox believe the bread and wine truly become Christ's body and blood; many Protestants see a holy remembrance; this is disputed.
fact: Some churches use grape juice instead of wine at communion.
fact: Catholic children often receive First Communion at about seven or eight.
fact: In confirmation a person confirms for themselves the promises made at their baptism, often with a bishop laying hands on them.
exercise: What is a sacrament? | An outward sign of God's grace given in church | A list of rules that every single Christian must keep | A prize given for learning the whole creed by heart | A song that is sung at the end of every service
exercise: How many sacraments do most Protestant churches count? | Two | Seven | Three | Twelve
exercise: How many sacraments do Catholics and Orthodox count? | Seven | Two | Twelve | One
exercise: Why are ordinary things like water and bread used in sacraments? | They become signs people can see of grace they cannot see | They are cheap, so churches can afford them | They are the only things allowed inside churches | They have magic powers on their own
exercise: In whose name is a person baptised? | The Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit | The vicar, the bishop and the Pope in Rome | Mary, Joseph and Jesus | Peter, Paul and John
exercise: What does the water in baptism stand for? | Washing away sin and starting a new life | Keeping the baby cool during the service | The flood in Noah's time destroying sin | A test of who can hold their breath
exercise: Which churches baptise babies? | Catholic, Orthodox, Anglican, Methodist and Presbyterian | Only Baptist and Pentecostal churches | No churches, it is against the Bible | Only churches in very hot countries
exercise: Who makes promises for a baby at an infant baptism? | The parents and godparents | The baby, using sign language | The whole choir, in a song | Only the bishop of the area
exercise: What makes a good godparent? | Someone who will help the child grow in faith | Someone who gives the most expensive presents | Someone who lives far away and never visits | Someone chosen because they are famous
exercise: What is believer's baptism? | Baptising people old enough to choose for themselves | Baptising only those people who were born into Christian families | Baptising babies with a few drops of water | Baptising people a second time each year
exercise: How are people usually baptised in believer's baptism? | By full immersion under the water | By a sprinkle of sand on the head | By a candle lit beside their name | By drinking a cup of holy water
exercise: What reason do churches that baptise babies give? | God's love comes before we can understand it | Babies are much too young to make any noise in church | Babies need the water to help them grow up strong | Adults are much too old to be baptised in church
exercise: What reason do churches that baptise only believers give? | Faith should be a personal choice | Babies are too heavy to hold in water | Children cannot be loved by God | Water is too cold for small babies
exercise: Which of these is NOT a name for Holy Communion? | Confirmation | The Eucharist | The Mass | The Lord's Supper
exercise: Why does communion share bread and wine? | Jesus asked his friends to at the Last Supper | It was the cheapest meal in Jerusalem | It began as a harvest festival custom | A bishop invented it in the Middle Ages for feast days
exercise: How do Catholic and many Protestant views of communion differ? | Catholics believe it truly becomes Christ's body; many Protestants see a holy remembrance | Catholics use bread only; Protestants use wine only | Catholics never take communion; Protestants take it daily | There is no difference; all churches agree exactly
exercise: At about what age do Catholic children often make their First Communion? | Seven or eight | One or two | Fifteen or sixteen | Twenty-one
exercise: What happens at confirmation? | People make their baptism promises their own | Babies are given their names for the first time | People are made into priests by the bishop | People promise never to sin again
test: «ur baby cousin got splashed with water so now she's magic lol». What do Christians believe baptism actually is? | A sign of God's grace and joining the Church | A spell that gives the baby magic powers | A bath that the church gives to every newborn baby in the town | A way to keep the baby from ever crying
test: Your Baptist friend says, «Baby baptism doesn't count, they didn't choose.» Your Anglican friend gets cross. What's the best-reasoned thing to say? | Both sides have serious reasons; it is a real dispute | The Baptist is right and the Anglican is just wrong about it | The Anglican is right and the Baptist is wrong | It doesn't matter, so stop talking about it
test: Tom, from a non-religious family, asks: «Why did they dunk that man right under the water?» What is the answer? | Believer's baptism by full immersion, a sign of new life | He fell into the pool by accident | It is a swimming test for new members | It is a punishment for missing church
test: At a friend's Mass you see people go up, but your friend's gran stays seated. A kid says «she's not allowed, she's bad». What's the fairer view? | There are many reasons; it is not ours to judge | Yes, only really good people may go up to the front | She must have simply forgotten the way up to the front | She is too old for communion now, it's for kids
test: «Catholics are weird, they think bread turns into a body,» sneers an older kid. Best respectful reply? | Catholics believe it truly becomes Christ's body; others see it differently | Yes, Catholics are weird and wrong | No, Catholics think the bread is just a snack | All Christians believe exactly that
test: Your sister says: «Confirmation's when the bishop makes you a grown-up.» Put her right. | You make your baptism promises your own | The bishop gives you a driving licence | You get your first name from the bishop | You are made into a priest that day
test: An olympiad judge asks: «Why might someone want to wait until they're older to be confirmed?» Best answer? | So the promise is truly their own, made with understanding | Because the bishop is too busy for children | Because older people get bigger presents | Because confirmation is only for adults by law in every country
test: Your godmother hasn't spoken to you about faith in years. Your brother says «she's useless, sack her». What fits the role best? | Talk to her; godparents are meant to help a child grow in faith | Yes, godparents can be fired like workers if they don't do their job | Godparents only have to send birthday cards | Godparents stop mattering after the baptism
test: «we have juice not wine at my church, so it's not real communion» says a friend. What's true? | Some churches use juice; it is still communion to them | Communion only counts if it is made with real red wine | Juice means it is just a snack time and not anything holy | Only Catholic churches have real communion, nobody else
test: A judge follows up: «Why use ordinary things like water and bread for something holy?» | Everyday things can become visible signs of God's grace | Holy things must always be expensive | Water and bread were the only food in church, so they had to use them | Ordinary things are used so nobody notices
test: Your friend says, «My church has seven sacraments and yours has two, so mine has more God.» Best reply? | Churches count differently, but all value God's grace | Seven is more than two, so his church does have more of God | Two is fewer, so it is purer and better | Sacraments are not about God at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a sacrament as understood by Christians? | An outward and visible sign of an inward grace bestowed by God | A code of conduct to which all believers must subscribe without exception | A recompense conferred upon proficiency in the catechism | A canticle sung at the conclusion of divine service
exam: Identify the principal ground upon which certain churches administer baptism solely to believers. | That faith ought to be the personal decision of the one baptised | That infants are of insufficient weight to be immersed | That infants are excluded from the love of God | That water is deemed injurious to infants
exam: Select the response consistent with Catholic teaching concerning the bread and wine of the Eucharist. | That they truly become the body and blood of Christ | That they remain solely a symbolic remembrance of the meal | That they are to be consumed exclusively by the clergy present | That they are partaken of upon Christmas Day alone each year
exam: Ascertain the purpose of the rite of confirmation. | The personal affirmation of the promises made at baptism | The conferral of a Christian name upon an infant | The ordination of the candidate to the priesthood | The undertaking henceforth to commit no sin whatsoever for life
